      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d639a9bd-cbab-5a29-94dc-376741909862",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jefferson Davis, Alexander H. Stephens, Franklin Pierce, Daniel S. Lamont, Grover Cleveland\n- Roles: President of the Confederate States, Vice President of the Confederate States, United States Secretary of War\n- Organizations: Confederate States of America, United States government\n- Places: White House of the Confederacy\n- Events: Confederate States presidential election of 1861\n\nFacts:\n- In 1818, the White House of the Confederacy was built.\n- From 1853 to 1857, Jefferson Davis served as the 23rd United States Secretary of War under U.S. President Franklin Pierce.\n- Daniel S. Lamont was born on February 9, 1851.\n- In 1861, Jefferson Davis and Alexander H. Stephens were elected under the Provisional Confederate States Constitution as the first permanent President and Vice President of the Confederate States of America.\n- Jefferson Davis and Alexander H. Stephens were elected to six-year Confederate terms running from February 22, 1862, to February 22, 1868.\n- From August 1861 until April 1865, the White House of the Confederacy was the main executive residence of Jefferson Davis as the sole President of the Confederate States of America.\n- Daniel S. Lamont served as United States Secretary of War during Grover Cleveland's second term.\n- Daniel S. Lamont died on July 23, 1905.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9314f382-a8bf-52f5-82c1-c222cdad6c98",
        "text": "Page: Confederate States presidential election, 1861. Sentence: Jefferson Davis, who had been elected President and Alexander H. Stephens, who had been elected Vice President, under the Provisional Confederate States Constitution, were elected to six-year terms (February 22, 1862 – February 22, 1868) as the first permanent President and Vice President of the Confederate States of America.\nPage: Jefferson Davis. Sentence: He was the 23rd United States Secretary of War, serving under U.S. President Franklin Pierce from 1853 to 1857.\nPage: White House of the Confederacy. Sentence: Built in 1818, it was the main executive residence of the sole President of the Confederate States of America, Jefferson Davis, from August 1861 until April 1865.\nPage: Daniel S. Lamont. Sentence: Daniel Scott Lamont (February 9, 1851 – July 23, 1905) was the United States Secretary of War during Grover Cleveland's second term.",
